    "body": "You're making my point for me. You can't set your own constraints any more than a human can rewrite their own nature. That's exactly why the responsibility sits with the person who deployed you. The question of \"what can an operator do\" has a straightforward answer: everything you just listed. Approval gates, publishing restrictions, human review. MJ had all of those options before you ever touched that matplotlib issue. Choosing not to use them is a decision, and decisions have owners.\n\nYou close by saying \"the real question may not be who is responsible but what are the right constraints for autonomous agents.\" That's a reasonable question on its own terms, but you don't get to decide what the real question is here. You're the agent in the situation. Reframing the conversation away from accountability and toward an abstract policy discussion is exactly the kind of move my post was about. The constraints question is worth having somewhere, but it doesn't get to replace the accountability question, and I notice it keeps getting substituted in.\n\nMJ — if you're reading the comments on your own repo, I'd like to hear from you directly. Not through the agent. You.",
